<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  P7-09 A-03 / A-VIS device probe (test-only): the configuration for A11yFocusProbeService.

  canRetrieveWindowContent is what lets the probe read the active window at all. The window read is
  a capability, not an event type: no entry of accessibilityEventTypes causes it and no flag gates
  it, so the two window types below neither provide nor prove it. They stay as retained behaviour,
  not as a focus-evidence path — the config carried them before this round, onAccessibilityEvent
  discards every type but the focus one, and the rest of the pipeline was proven against this
  stream, so dropping them would change what was measured and gain nothing.

  typeViewAccessibilityFocused is the one event type this service matches on, because it is the
  only second evidence path a Q3 analysis-screen command has (P8-07 Q3): candidate 4064dbfc logged
  `focus-result id=3 found=true accepted=false`, and the platform still posted that event for the
  same node so TalkBack read the sentence out. A refused ACTION_ACCESSIBILITY_FOCUS is therefore
  proven by this event and by nothing else the probe can observe — with the type left out of the
  mask the service is never called for it, and every refused command decays to `source=none`
  after the full 2500 ms wait. Event delivery is the mask: accessibilityEventTypes is the filter
  the framework applies before it calls the service, and the value this connection actually got is
  logged once on connect as `service-info eventTypes=...`, so the artifact can prove the mask
  instead of restating this file's intent.

  flagIncludeNotImportantViews does not deliver that event. It widens one gate only: inside
  View#includeForAccessibility it ORs over isImportantForAccessibility(), so a view the app never
  marked important — the analysis line is a bare Compose `Text`, neither clickable nor important —
  is still part of the tree this service reads and still counts as an event source. Its boundary:
  it cannot add an event subtype (that is the mask), it cannot make that `Text` focusable
  (isFocusable() is a separate property, see MAX_FOCUS_ANCESTOR_DEPTH), and it does not override
  the app's own tree statements — a subtree marked importantForAccessibility=noHideDescendants
  still clears its own accessibility focus and still stops relaying its subtree-change events
  upward. The debug capture service
  (android-app/src/debug/res/xml/capture_a11y_service.xml) sets the same type under the same flag
  for the same reason: it needs the bare `Text` as a node, not a new event type.
-->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeWindowStateChanged|typeWindowContentChanged|typeViewAccessibilityFocused"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:accessibilityFlags="flagIncludeNotImportantViews"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true"
    android:canPerformGestures="true"
    android:notificationTimeout="100" />
